                    Design: We will implement test-time                         
                    augmentation by averaging predictions                       
                    between original and horizontally                           
                    flipped images. During validation, for                      
                    each batch we compute predictions on                        
                    both original images and their                              
                    horizontal flips, average the predicted                     
                    probabilities, and use these averaged                       
                    probabilities to compute ROC AUC. We                        
                    also extend this TTA logic to the                           
                    predict function so that test-time                          
                    predictions are similarly averaged                          
                    across two views. This simple TTA often                     
                    improves model robustness and can lift                      
                    the validation ROC AUC slightly.                            
                    Results: The training script executed                       
                    successfully without errors. The model                      
                    was trained using a DenseNet121                             
                    architecture with CutMix augmentation                       
                    and tested with test-time augmentation                      
                    (horizontal flip). The validation ROC                       
                    AUC achieved was 0.8894, indicating                         
                    good classification performance for                         
                    distinguishing malignant from benign                        
                    skin lesions.                                               
